OpenWrt Fork Workflow
=====================

Purpose
-------
This document describes the recommended Git workflow for maintaining a fork of
the OpenWrt repository while keeping it synchronized with upstream development.

This approach keeps the fork clean and makes upgrading OpenWrt versions easy.

Repository Structure
--------------------

GitHub fork:
    https://github.com/<YOURUSER>/openwrt

Remotes:

    origin   -> your GitHub fork
    upstream -> official OpenWrt repository

Branches:

    main                upstream development branch
    openwrt-25.12       upstream release branch
    pw-hub-25.12        local working branch for custom modifications


Initial Setup
-------------

1. Fork the OpenWrt repository on GitHub

    https://github.com/openwrt/openwrt


2. Clone your fork

    git clone git@github.com:<YOURUSER>/openwrt.git
    cd openwrt


3. Add the upstream OpenWrt repository

    git remote add upstream https://github.com/openwrt/openwrt.git


4. Fetch upstream branches

    git fetch upstream


Create a Local Tracking Branch
------------------------------

Example for OpenWrt 25.12:

    git checkout -b openwrt-25.12 upstream/openwrt-25.12


Push this branch to your fork:

    git push origin openwrt-25.12


Create Your Working Branch
--------------------------

Do NOT modify the upstream tracking branch directly.

Instead create a working branch:

    git checkout -b pw-hub-25.12 openwrt-25.12


All custom work should occur in this branch.


Keeping Your Fork Updated
-------------------------

Update the upstream branch:

    git checkout openwrt-25.12
    git fetch upstream
    git pull --rebase upstream openwrt-25.12


Update your working branch:

    git checkout pw-hub-25.12
    git rebase openwrt-25.12


Push updates to GitHub:

    git push --force-with-lease


Using Rebase Instead of Merge
-----------------------------

Always use rebase when syncing with upstream.

Correct:

    git pull --rebase upstream openwrt-25.12

Avoid:

    git merge upstream/openwrt-25.12

Rebasing keeps the commit history clean and avoids unnecessary merge commits.


Optional Git Alias
------------------

You can simplify upstream syncing by adding this alias to ~/.gitconfig:

    [alias]
    sync = !"git fetch upstream && git rebase upstream/$(git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD)"


After adding this alias you can update your branch with:

    git sync


Recommended Patch Strategy
--------------------------

For large OpenWrt customizations it is often easier to maintain changes as patches.

Example structure:

    patches/
        001-feature.patch
        002-driver.patch
        003-package.patch


Apply patches with:

    git am patches/*


This approach makes upgrading between OpenWrt versions significantly easier.


Recommended Vendor Branch Layout
--------------------------------

    openwrt
    ├── main
    ├── openwrt-25.12
    └── pw-hub-25.12

    openwrt-25.12
        Pure upstream tracking branch

    pw-hub-25.12
        PredictWind DataHub firmware modifications


Typical Workflow
----------------

Update upstream:

    git checkout openwrt-25.12
    git sync


Rebase your modifications:

    git checkout pw-hub-25.12
    git rebase openwrt-25.12


Push your updates:

    git push --force-with-lease


Notes
-----

Do not modify upstream tracking branches directly.

Always develop on a separate working branch.

Rebasing keeps the history clean and makes long-term maintenance much easier.
